WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.890
There is a sentence you say at two in the morning, when nothing can be changed: if only.

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:12.100
You call it conscience — but what if it is only a way of hurting yourself for a past that no longer exists?

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.710
And if you tore that sentence out, what would be left to keep you honest?

4
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:31.280
Bu kanal çeviri kanalı: içerik üretilmiyor, aynı günün felsefe bölümü İngilizceye çevriliyor.

5
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:47.410
Bugünün kaynağı `felsefe.txt` (03:47) ve çevirisi `philosophy.txt` olarak 06:14'te zaten üretilmiş; aşağıdaki metin odur — kaynağa birebir sadık, işaret satırları korunmuş, sayılar yazıyla.

6
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:59.060
Tek düzeltme: kaynakta var olup çeviride düşmüş 3 boş satırı yerine koydum (paragraf 28/29, 49/50, 68/69 arası).

7
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.950
```

8
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.400
That sentence that arrives when you're staring at the ceiling in the middle of the night:

9
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:13.530
"if only." Why are you still handing down a sentence for something you cannot change — and is that punishment actually making you a better person?

10
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:19.100
In this episode we ask whether regret is a compass, or a trap you've set for yourself.

11
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.990
It's a little past three in the morning.

12
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:36.200
You're lying on your back, your eyes fixed in the dark on that never-changing emptiness of the ceiling.

13
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.850
You have to be at work tomorrow morning; you should sleep.

14
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.220
But your mind isn't sleeping.

15
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:50.350
Your mind is replaying a scene: an answer given ten years ago, a message never sent, a sheet of paper you signed, or didn't.

16
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.960
And over that scene it keeps insisting on the same two syllables.

17
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.650
If only.

18
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:08.020
Turkish borrowed this word from Persian; the original is "kâşkî." In Arabic, the same sigh comes out as "yâ leyte." Almost every language has a short,

19
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:15.030
nearly childlike formula that does this job — because what's being expressed isn't complicated, only unbearable.

20
00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:20.120
The human being is the only creature that can see a thing that never happened as though it had.

21
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:26.170
"If only" is the bill for that gift.

22
00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:30.460
If we want to lay out the anatomy of regret, we need three parts.

23
00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:36.030
First, a fork in the road: at least two doors, and the fact that you chose one of them.

24
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:42.720
Second, an outcome: one you don't like, one that hurts, one whose account you haven't settled.

25
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:51.490
Third — and this is the part that truly burns — imagination: the capacity to bring to life what lay behind the door you didn't choose.

26
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:55.140
Without that third part there is pain, but no regret.

27
00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:03.270
Someone whose house collapses in an earthquake suffers the pain of the ruin, but does not regret it, because the fault line was not their decision.

28
00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:12.520
But if, that morning before leaving the house, they had wondered whether to have that wall reinforced and then dropped the idea, something entirely different mixes into the pain.

29
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.210
Regret is the price of agency.

30
00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:24.300
Someone who believes they never decided anything regrets nothing — which is why some people turn never deciding into a strategy for living.

31
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.030
Does it work?

32
00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.400
We'll come to that.

33
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.170
There's a measurement that shows just how arbitrarily imagination operates here.

34
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:40.340
Thomas Gilovich, Victoria Medvec and Scott Madey examined footage from the nineteen ninety-two Barcelona Olympics;

35
00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:46.710
they had observers who didn't know the results rate the athletes' facial expressions at the moment of the medal and on the podium.

36
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:55.000
The picture that emerged looks irrational, yet all of us recognize it: silver medalists were unhappier than bronze medalists.

37
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.970
Objectively, silver is better.

38
00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:10.460
But the film playing in the silver medalist's mind is "I almost came first," while the film playing in the bronze medalist's mind is "I almost didn't medal at all." What determines happiness isn't the medal in your hand;

39
00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.110
it's the alternative your mind produces all by itself.

40
00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:19.040
And the production of those alternatives isn't random either — it has a direction.

41
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:31.970
A now-classic question from Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky goes like this: Paul considers swapping the shares he holds for another company's shares, decides against it, and stays put.

42
00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.340
George swaps his.

43
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.150
Both lose the same amount, twelve hundred dollars.

44
00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.360
Which one regrets it more?

45
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.290
More than ninety percent of participants point to George: the one who acted.

46
00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:54.860
Because inaction is easy to defend — you can say, "I didn't do anything." Action demands a signature, and a signature means responsibility.

47
00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.390
But time reverses this picture.

48
00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:04.720
When Gilovich and Medvec asked people to look back over their entire lives and name their greatest regrets, the list changed:

49
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:11.730
regret over the things they hadn't done outweighed regret over the things they had — in the studies, by roughly a factor of two.

50
00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:17.780
Even a wrong marriage turns into a story over time, becomes tellable, yields its lesson.

51
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.950
The book never written, though, never closes.

52
00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:25.720
A mistake you made has an ending; a thing you never did has none.

53
00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:30.330
Like an unclosed parenthesis, it takes the rest of your life inside it.

54
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.940
And this is exactly where that moment of staring at the ceiling hurts.

55
00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:41.790
Because what's at stake there isn't a single decision; what's at stake is the person you could have been and weren't.

56
00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:46.240
So — if you had chosen the other door, would you have been saved?

57
00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:54.530
In eighteen forty-three in Copenhagen, a two-volume book appears under the pen name Victor Eremita: Enten-Eller, that is, Either/Or.

58
00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.380
Its author is Søren Kierkegaard.

59
00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:04.710
Among the short, disjointed notes at the start of the first volume lies a sequence of sentences that breaks the modern person's spine.

60
00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:10.920
Marry, he says, and you will regret it; don't marry, and you will regret that too.

61
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:17.770
Laugh at the world's follies, and you will regret it; weep over them, and you will regret that too.

62
00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:23.340
Hang yourself, and you will regret it; don't hang yourself, and you will regret that too.

63
00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:29.070
And in the end he leaves this as the summation of all life's wisdom: either way, you will regret it.

64
00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:33.360
This is not a display of pessimism but a diagnosis of a structure.

65
00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:37.330
To choose is, by definition, to kill.

66
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.820
To walk one road means closing off all the others.

67
00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:50.390
When you chose that department at university, four departments you didn't choose died; when you stayed in that city, your lives in the cities you never moved to died;

68
00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:55.320
when you stayed with that person — or left them — one more possibility was buried.

69
00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.650
Every biography is also a graveyard.

70
00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:02.140
And the residents of that graveyard never grow old.

71
00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:07.230
Here's the most unfair thing about the life you never lived: it never gets tired.

72
00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:13.760
Real life has tax debt, mornings when your back aches, dishes in the sink, a relative who wears you out.

73
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:17.090
The alternative life in your head has none of that.

74
00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:23.780
Its tires never go flat, its child never spikes a fever in the night, its boss is never an idiot.

75
00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:32.870
In the trial you stage in your mind, real life sits in the defendant's chair with every one of its flaws on display, while the alternative life never takes the stand at all.

76
00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.840
In a court like that, the life that didn't happen wins every time.

77
00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:43.370
That's the mathematical trick of regret: we compare what we lived through to the advertising poster of what we didn't.

78
00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:46.380
Modernity industrialized that trick.

79
00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.950
What Barry Schwartz examines in The Paradox of Choice is exactly this.

80
00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:57.840
As the number of options goes up, satisfaction doesn't rise — it falls.

81
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:06.450
Schwartz and his colleagues divide people into two camps: those who want to find the very best, and those who take something good enough and get on with their lives.

82
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:14.260
The ones chasing the best may well end up with better outcomes, but they report more regret, less satisfaction, more comparing.

83
00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:20.630
Because the moment you say "the best," you leave behind you an endless queue of options you didn't choose.

84
00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:30.680
When the supermarket jam shelf holds forty varieties, the jar you pick stops being simply a good jam and becomes a verdict handed down against thirty-nine other possibilities.

85
00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:38.330
The same logic runs in the dating app on your phone, on the job listings site, in the twenty tabs you open while apartment hunting.

86
00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:43.580
Our grandparents had little regret, because they had few doors.

87
00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:48.670
And here we meet the darker face of the question in our title.

88
00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:52.640
When regret leads nowhere, it turns into a machine.

89
00:08:52.640 --> 00:09:04.290
The thing Susan Nolen-Hoeksema spent decades studying is called rumination, and the most honest image for it is the way cattle chew the cud — bringing the same mouthful back up and grinding it again.

90
00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:14.020
Her finding is unambiguous: people do this because they mistake it for a form of problem solving, when in fact rumination is the opposite of problem solving,

91
00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:18.630
and it doesn't ease depression — it deepens it and draws it out.

92
00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:22.280
You can catch the difference in the grammar of a single sentence.

93
00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:31.850
The mind that asks "why me, why did this happen to me, why am I like this" is spinning in place; it's asking a question that will accept no answer,

94
00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:36.300
because it isn't really looking for an answer — it's handing down a punishment.

95
00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:43.950
The mind that asks "here's what I did in that situation, and here's what I'll do if it comes around again" is walking forward.

96
00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.960
The first one stares at the ceiling.

97
00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.770
The second one looks at tomorrow morning.

98
00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:58.260
So the question becomes: is there a way to say yes to what has already happened — to say yes without lying to yourself?

99
00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:06.870
In eighteen eighty-two, Nietzsche opens the fourth part of Die fröhliche Wissenschaft — The Gay Science — with a short passage written for the new year.

100
00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:17.720
In it he makes himself a wish: from now on, his only desire is to wage no more war against what is; to learn to see even what strikes him as ugly as beautiful; to stop laying blame.

101
00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:23.930
And he gives this stance a name: amor fati, the love of fate.

102
00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:28.380
In a later passage of the same book, he turns it into a test.

103
00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:40.510
Imagine, he says, that one day in your loneliest loneliness a demon steals up beside you and says: you will have to live this life again, and countless times again; nothing new in it, every pain, every joy,

104
00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:46.720
every small thought, even this moonlight you see now and this spider, will come back in the very same order.

105
00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:48.930
What do you do?

106
00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:53.860
Do you grind your teeth and curse the demon, or can you say, "I want this again"?

107
00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.950
Nietzsche frames the question not as a recipe for happiness but as a set of scales.

108
00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:02.440
It measures the real mark you'd give your own life.

109
00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:08.650
A few years later, in Thus Spoke Zarathustra, he rebuilds the same idea in the language of the will.

110
00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:15.500
The deepest wound in the human will is that it cannot will backward; the past is the stone the will can get no grip on.

111
00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:27.630
Deliverance lies in turning "it was so" into "thus I willed it." And in one of his last books, he boils the formula for human greatness down to a single sentence: to want nothing to be other than it is — not ahead,

112
00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:31.120
not behind, not for all eternity.

113
00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:41.970
It's worth remembering that the man who said this was sick, nearly blind, drifting from one boarding house to the next with relentless headaches, writing books nobody bought, and profoundly alone.

114
00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:46.580
Amor fati is not a declaration of pleasure; it's a discipline.

115
00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:54.550
It isn't something said by a person who finds it easy to love their life — it's something said by a person looking for a technique that would let them.

116
00:11:54.550 --> 00:12:03.160
Two hundred years before Nietzsche, in Amsterdam, another man who made his living grinding lenses drew a far colder conclusion from the same problem.

117
00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:13.050
In the fourth part of the Ethics, published in sixteen seventy-seven after Spinoza's death, there's a proposition: regret is not a virtue, it does not arise from reason;

118
00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:17.020
the person who regrets what they have done is twice wretched.

119
00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:20.830
The logic is mercilessly clean.

120
00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:30.400
First you gave in to a misguided desire, and now you're giving in to your own whip; you're taking two separate losses in two separate places, and you're growing stronger from neither.

121
00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:35.330
But the really interesting part of Spinoza is the note he attaches to this proposition.

122
00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:45.540
He says: because human beings so rarely live by reason, feelings such as humility and repentance — like fear — do more good than harm in practice.

123
00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:55.270
In other words, the philosopher is saying this: for an ideal being, repentance is a needless pain; for a real human being, it is a brake that actually works.

124
00:12:55.270 --> 00:13:06.280
Perhaps the answer to the question in our title lies inside that distinction: repentance is unnecessary for a being governed wholly by reason; for beings like us, it is, for now, necessary.

125
00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:10.410
Now let us look at the same matter in the language of these lands.

126
00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:19.820
The eighteenth-century thinker İbrahim Hakkı of Erzurum, known for his Marifetnâme, wrote a poem known as the Tefviznâme, and it is still recited from memory today.

127
00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:23.470
Tefviz means handing the matter back to its owner.

128
00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:32.880
The poem opens by saying that things which look like misfortune can be turned to good; it counsels a person not to fall into vain imaginings, not to let the heart grow clouded;

129
00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:41.330
and at the end of every stanza it returns to the same two lines: let us see what the Lord will do — whatever He does, He does beautifully.

130
00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:49.300
The verbs are striking: he says "let us see." Not a fixed piece of knowledge, but a judgment still waiting to unfold.

131
00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:55.190
What is asked here is acceptance of fate, but without finishing the sentence of fate inside your own head.

132
00:13:55.190 --> 00:14:01.720
To keep acceptance from being confused with passivity, this tradition draws a very clear line within itself.

133
00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:06.650
That famous saying goes: tie up your camel first, then trust in God.

134
00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:14.780
The station of acceptance in Sufism looks at what is finished and done; it does not tie a person's hands against what is still to come.

135
00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:19.070
Acceptance toward what has happened, will toward what is yet to happen.

136
00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:24.800
The moment you lose that distinction, both amor fati and trust in God turn into a narcotic:

137
00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:35.490
they become a cover for calling oppression "fate," for calling neglect "it was written this way." To love what has happened is not the same as refusing to intervene in what is happening.

138
00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:40.580
And to be honest, there is one more limit to saying yes to the past.

139
00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:48.230
A person can say of their own suffering, "I willed it this way." They have no right to say that about someone else's suffering.

140
00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:53.160
Amor fati is ethical in the first person singular and cruel in the third.

141
00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:56.810
Accepting the past silences the if only.

142
00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.860
But something is left missing.

143
00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:04.750
Because repentance is not only a pain to be soothed; it is also a message, a notification.

144
00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:10.640
And in the thought of these lands there is a very finely tuned mechanism for what to do with that notification.

145
00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:15.730
In Arabic, tevbe — repentance — literally means to turn back.

146
00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:24.500
A short saying found in the hadith sources goes to the heart of the matter: remorse is repentance; that is, the feeling of regret is the turning itself.

147
00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:28.790
At first glance this seems to say that the emotion is enough.

148
00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:39.320
But al-Ghazali, in his work İhyâu Ulûmi'd-dîn, The Revival of the Religious Sciences, divides repentance into three parts: knowledge, state, and deed.

149
00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:43.770
Knowledge comes first: truly seeing where what you did is taking you.

150
00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:49.180
Out of that knowledge a state is born, and that state is remorse — the heart's pain.

151
00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:56.030
And out of that pain a deed is born: stopping, making amends, resolving never to return to it.

152
00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:06.080
In the architecture of the Revival, remorse is the middle link in the chain; without knowledge in front of it and action behind it, the chain snaps and all you are left holding is the pain.

153
00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:11.650
This may be the clearest statement of the distinction we have been looking for throughout this whole episode.

154
00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:16.580
Repentance, with knowledge placed before it and action after it, is discipline.

155
00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:22.630
Without the knowledge in front, it is only shame; without the action behind, it is only torture.

156
00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:27.240
The same feeling, in two different chains, becomes two different things.

157
00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.530
How the Qur'an uses the words if only is also remarkable in this light.

158
00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:37.740
In the chapter of Mary a day is given a name: the day of longing, the day of regret.

159
00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:46.990
In the chapter of the Criterion there is a man biting his hands; twice he says if only, once about the road he took and once about the companion he chose.

160
00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:57.680
In the chapter of the Tidings a voice is heard saying, "if only I had been dust." Every if only in the text is an if only that comes far too late; none of them can be made good.

161
00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:01.330
Yet those words are being recited to someone alive today.

162
00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:06.420
Which means the religious text turns future regret into the compass of a present decision.

163
00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:14.390
The direction of the if only has been reversed: a voice that looked backward and wounded has become a voice that looks forward and warns.

164
00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:20.440
Right alongside this, within the very same tradition, stands a clear prohibition aimed at rumination.

165
00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:31.770
In a saying transmitted by Muslim, a person is warned against saying, about a matter already finished, "if I had done it this way, that would not have happened" — because the word "if" opens the devil's door.

166
00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:39.580
Notice: self-examination is not forbidden; in the same sources, taking account of oneself is explicitly recommended.

167
00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:45.310
What is forbidden is the "if" sentence built on top of something that can no longer be changed.

168
00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:54.720
Fourteenth-century ethics and twenty-first-century psychology draw, oddly enough, the very same line: do not chew it over — settle the account.

169
00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.850
Modern research doesn't treat regret as a pointless malfunction either.

170
00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:09.540
Neal Roese's work on counterfactual thinking shows that regret is a functional signal: from it you learn where you measured something wrong, what you overvalued, which of your own values you betrayed.

171
00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:14.470
And there's a practical test for telling whether the signal is helping you or harming you.

172
00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:18.440
Can you turn your regret into a single forward-looking sentence?

173
00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:29.130
"The next time an offer like that comes along, I'll take it." "The next time we fight, I'll call before I go home." If it translates, that regret is a lesson.

174
00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:38.540
If it doesn't, what you're holding isn't a lesson — it's a small courtroom you've built for yourself, and since you are both the judge and the defendant, the verdict never comes.

175
00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:41.870
So where do we hear this signal most clearly?

176
00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:51.600
Bronnie Ware, an Australian palliative care nurse, gathered the notes from her years spent at the bedsides of the dying into a book called The Top Five Regrets of the Dying.

177
00:18:51.600 --> 00:19:01.650
The sentence at the top of that list is this: I wish I'd had the courage to be myself — to live my own life, rather than the life others expected of me.

178
00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:12.820
The rest belong to the same family: I wish I hadn't worked so hard, I wish I'd been able to say what I felt, I wish I hadn't let my friendships fade, I wish I'd allowed myself to be happier.

179
00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:16.150
Not one of them is the confession of a mistake.

180
00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:20.760
Every one of them is an absence: the thing left undone.

181
00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:23.770
And here an unsettling symmetry emerges.

182
00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:29.180
Almost every regret voiced at the end of a life concerns something that could still be done today.

183
00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:33.310
Which means that list isn't a lament — it's a recipe.

184
00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:38.720
The present-day equivalent of a deathbed "I wish" is a list of acts of courage.

185
00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:49.090
And this reveals that regret's cruellest quality and its most useful quality are one and the same thing: regret reminds us that time runs in only one direction.

186
00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:54.980
Repentance repairs your relationship with the past, but it does not give back the lost year.

187
00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:59.590
Acceptance eases the pain, but it does not make what never happened happen.

188
00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:02.920
No retroactive surgery is possible.

189
00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:07.690
The only thing that is possible is running the same mechanism forwards.

190
00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:15.500
So the task of the person staring at the ceiling at three in the morning is not to stop the film playing in their head; it's to turn the film around.

191
00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:27.310
The question "why did I do that, that day" has no answer, because that day's circumstances, its knowledge, its fear are all gone now; the person asking the question has never met the person who could answer it.

192
00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:33.680
But the question "what will I do tomorrow at nine o'clock" does have an answer — and that answer can be given tonight.

193
00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:36.690
And usually it's something small.

194
00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:39.060
A phone call.

195
00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.750
An apology.

196
00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:44.760
An application put off for years.

197
00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:52.410
The next morning won't erase the mistake you made ten years ago; but it will erase one line from the list of regrets you'd have ten years from now.

198
00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:56.860
That brings us to the end of today's episode here on Neuron Philosophy.

199
00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.870
Until we meet again, in a new story.

